'''Kawara Shrine''' (かわらじんじゃ) is an {{jalink|auxiliary shrine|Q1071160|sessha}} of {{jalink|Ise Grand Shrine|Q687168}}{{jalink|Kōtaijingū|Q11581011}} (Inner Shrine).

=== Overview ===
It ranks 20th among the 27 auxiliary shrines of the Inner Shrine.<ref>Ujiyamada City Office, ed. (1929): pp. 9–11</ref>

It is a {{jalink|shrine|Q845945}} designated by {{jalink|Yamatohime-no-Mikoto|Q4388732}}, and the {{jalink|enshrined deity|Q11591100}} is said to be the {{jalink|guardian deity|Q131278957}} of the {{jalink|Miyagawa River (Mie Prefecture)|Q6884164}}.<ref name="ic">Ise Bunka-sha, ed. (2008): p. 70</ref><ref name="gk">Gakken Publishing (2013): p. 71</ref>

The shrine is located on the east bank of the Miyagawa River,<ref name="gk"/> at 1559 Sensui, aza Sōchi, Sōchi-chō, {{jalink|Ise City|Q328067}}, {{jalink|Mie Prefecture|Q128196}}.<ref name="tm">Tatsumi Publishing (2013): p. 76</ref> The place name “Sōchi” means “marshland,” indicating that wetlands from the Miyagawa once existed there.<ref name="gk"/> There are also records stating that rice was offered from Sōchi to the Inner Shrine.<ref name="sk6">Shikinaisha Research Association, ed. (1990): p. 346</ref>

The shrine name appears as “Kawara Shrine” in the *Kōtaijingū Gishikichō*, while other sources list it as “Kawara Shrine” or “Kawara-sha.” Records state that local residents referred to it as “Senzui” (river water, spring water), “Odago,” or “Yagura.”<ref name="sk5">Shikinaisha Research Association, ed. (1990): p. 345</ref>

=== Precincts ===
The shrine grounds cover an {{jalink|area|Q11500}} of 3,219 {{jalink|square meters|Q25343|m<sup>2</sup>}}.<ref name="sk7">Shikinaisha Research Association, ed. (1990): p. 347</ref> The {{jalink|precincts|Q11429002}} form a tranquil space filled with dappled sunlight, birdsong, and the murmur of the river.<ref name="ic"/> Trees such as {{jalink|Japanese cypress|Q917187}}, {{jalink|camphor tree|Q158722}}, {{jalink|oak|Q796117}}, {{jalink|Machilus thunbergii|Q5792073}}, and {{jalink|Ilex latifolia|Q2706373}} grow densely within the grounds.<ref name="sk7"/>

=== Enshrined deity ===
The enshrined deity is {{jalink|Tsukuyomi|Q595520|Tsukuyomi no Mikoto no Mitama}}.<ref name="ic"/> This is the same {{jalink|deity (Shinto)|Q524158|kami}} worshipped at the {{jalink|Tsukiyomi-no-miya}} and {{jalink|Tsukiyomi-no-miya (Yoru-no-miya)|Q11516023}}, among the 125 shrines of Ise Grand Shrine.<ref name="tm"/> While Tsukuyomi is generally regarded as a {{jalink|moon|Q405}} deity, the moon and {{jalink|water|Q283}} are considered closely related, and in the context of Kawara Shrine, Tsukuyomi no Mikoto no Mitama is regarded as a river deity.<ref name="ic"/>

In addition to sharing the same enshrined deity, there is a theory that Sōchi-chō, where Kawara Shrine is located, and Nakamura-chō, where Tsukiyomi Shrine is located, were both deeply connected through the {{jalink|Arakida clan|Q11618716}}.<ref name="sk6"/>

=== History ===
According to tradition, {{jalink|Yamatohime-no-Mikoto|Q4388732}} was welcomed here by Ōwakako-no-Mikoto and traveled down the Miyagawa River by boat.<ref name="ic"/> Regarding its establishment, based on the definition of auxiliary shrines of Ise Grand Shrine, it is believed to have been founded before the compilation of the *{{jalink|Engishiki Jinmyōchō|Q11064932}}*, that is, before the 5th year of {{jalink|Enchō (era)|Q1327274|Enchō}} ({{jalink|927|Q30470}}).<ref name="ic2">Ise Bunka-sha, ed. (2008): p. 22</ref> According to the *Daijingū-shiki*, it was designated as one of the shrines involved in repairs by the {{jalink|chief priest|Q11453500}}, and it received offerings during the Kinensai and Kannamesai festivals.<ref name="sk5"/> However, during the {{jalink|Sengoku period (Japan)|Q204023|Sengoku period}}, {{jalink|rituals|Q11546824}} were discontinued,<ref name="tm"/> and the shrine grounds fell into ruin.<ref name="sk5"/> The hereditary priests ({{jalink|hafuri|Q11453500|hafuri}}) who had been appointed until then also died out.<ref name="sk6"/>

In the 3rd year of {{jalink|Kanbun|Q1146228}} ({{jalink|1663|Q7577}}), {{jalink|Kawabe Kiyonaga|Q92924151|Kawabe Kiyonaga}} restored the shrine at Sōchi.<ref name="sk5"/> On the 9th day of the 9th month (lunar calendar; {{jalink|Gregorian calendar|Q12138}}: {{jalink|October 9|Q2923}}, 1663), the {{jalink|shrine buildings|Q11453500}} were constructed, and on the 12th day of the 12th month (lunar calendar; Gregorian: {{jalink|January 10|Q2243}}, {{jalink|1664|Q7581}}), the deity was ceremonially transferred ({{jalink|sengu|Q24892914}}).<ref name="sk5"/> At that time, families that had formerly served as hereditary priests were sought out and reinstated.<ref name="sk6"/> During the restoration, local traditions and place names were referenced, but there is also a theory that Takehana Shrine in present-day Takegahana-chō, Ise City, or {{jalink|Kawarabuchi Shrine|Q17209232}} in {{jalink|Fun'e (Ise City)|Q17213313}} may have been the original site.<ref name="tm"/> Since a subsequent deity transfer on the 29th day of the 3rd month of the 7th year of {{jalink|Genroku|Q1325001}} (lunar calendar; Gregorian: {{jalink|April 23|Q2527}}, {{jalink|1694|Q7694}}), the shrine site has not moved.<ref name="sk5"/>

The shrine buildings were rebuilt in November {{jalink|1908|Q2056}} ({{jalink|Meiji|Q3125096}} 41).<ref>Ujiyamada City Office, ed. (1929): p. 11</ref> They were subsequently rebuilt again in May {{jalink|1960|Q3754}} ({{jalink|Shōwa|Q327567}} 35), and repairs were carried out in September {{jalink|1983|Q2434}} (Shōwa 58).<ref name="sk6"/>

=== Rituals ===
During the Kinensai, {{jalink|Tsukinamisai|Q11516161}} (June and December), {{jalink|Kannamesai|Q11255045}}, {{jalink|Niinamesai|Q11501518}}, and extraordinary festivals, {{jalink|Shinto priests|Q60523}} present {{jalink|offerings|Q11591045|shinsen}} and {{jalink|heihaku|Q11481885}} before the shrine. For rites such as the {{jalink|Saitan-sai|Q11546824}}, remote worship is performed at the Inner Shrine’s Gojōden Hall.<ref name="sk6"/>

=== Access ===
The shrine is situated in a forest slightly off {{jalink|Mie Prefectural Route 22 (Ise–Minamishima Line)|Q11357908}}.<ref>Shikinaisha Research Association, ed. (1990): pp. 346–347</ref>

* About a 12-minute walk (700 {{jalink|meters|Q11573|m}}) from the “Sōchi” {{jalink|bus stop|Q953806}} on a {{jalink|Mie Kōtsū|Q6843516}} {{jalink|route bus|Q1373492}}.<ref name="ic"/> Enter the alley opposite {{jalink|Ise Municipal Sōchi Elementary School|Q11379036}} near the bus stop and follow the rice paddies to reach the precincts.<ref name="ic"/>
* Approximately 5.5 {{jalink|kilometers|Q828224|km}} from {{jalink|Ise-shi Station|Q6079748}} on the {{jalink|JR Sangū Line|Q872023}} of {{jalink|Central Japan Railway Company|Q513679}} or the {{jalink|Kintetsu Yamada Line|Q900630}} of {{jalink|Kintetsu|Q1531085}}.<ref name="sk5"/>

