'''Kawara Shrine''' (かわらじんじゃ) is an {{ill|lt=auxiliary shrine|Q1071160|WD=Q1071160|lt=sessha|1=Setsumatsusha}} of {{ill|lt=Ise Grand Shrine|Q687168|WD=Q687168|1=Ise Grand Shrine}}{{ill|lt=Kōtaijingū|Q11581011|WD=Q11581011|1=Kōtai Jingū}} (Inner Shrine).

=== Overview ===
It ranks 20th among the 27 auxiliary shrines of the Inner Shrine.<ref>Ujiyamada City Office, ed. (1929): pp. 9–11</ref>

It is a {{ill|lt=shrine|Q845945|WD=Q845945|1=Shinto shrine}} designated by {{ill|lt=Yamatohime-no-Mikoto|Q4388732|WD=Q4388732|1=Yamatohime-no-mikoto}}, and the {{ill|lt=enshrined deity|Q11591100|WD=Q11591100|1=Saijin}} is said to be the {{ill|lt=guardian deity|Q131278957|WD=Q131278957|1=Guardian deities}} of the {{ill|lt=Miyagawa River (Mie Prefecture)|Q6884164|WD=Q6884164|1=Miya River (Mie)}}.<ref name="ic">Ise Bunka-sha, ed. (2008): p. 70</ref><ref name="gk">Gakken Publishing (2013): p. 71</ref>

The shrine is located on the east bank of the Miyagawa River,<ref name="gk"/> at 1559 Sensui, aza Sōchi, Sōchi-chō, {{ill|lt=Ise City|Q328067|WD=Q328067|1=Ise, Mie}}, {{ill|lt=Mie Prefecture|Q128196|WD=Q128196|1=Mie Prefecture}}.<ref name="tm">Tatsumi Publishing (2013): p. 76</ref> The place name “Sōchi” means “marshland,” indicating that wetlands from the Miyagawa once existed there.<ref name="gk"/> There are also records stating that rice was offered from Sōchi to the Inner Shrine.<ref name="sk6">Shikinaisha Research Association, ed. (1990): p. 346</ref>

The shrine name appears as “Kawara Shrine” in the *Kōtaijingū Gishikichō*, while other sources list it as “Kawara Shrine” or “Kawara-sha.” Records state that local residents referred to it as “Senzui” (river water, spring water), “Odago,” or “Yagura.”<ref name="sk5">Shikinaisha Research Association, ed. (1990): p. 345</ref>

=== Precincts ===
The shrine grounds cover an {{ill|lt=area|Q11500|WD=Q11500|1=Area}} of 3,219 {{ill|lt=square meters|Q25343|WD=Q25343|lt=m<sup>2</sup>|1=Square metre}}.<ref name="sk7">Shikinaisha Research Association, ed. (1990): p. 347</ref> The {{ill|lt=precincts|Q11429002|WD=Q11429002|1=keidai}} form a tranquil space filled with dappled sunlight, birdsong, and the murmur of the river.<ref name="ic"/> Trees such as {{ill|lt=Japanese cypress|Q917187|WD=Q917187|1=Chamaecyparis obtusa}}, {{ill|lt=camphor tree|Q158722|WD=Q158722|1=Camphora officinarum}}, {{ill|lt=oak|Q796117|WD=Q796117|1=Cyclobalanopsis}}, {{ill|lt=Machilus thunbergii|Q5792073|WD=Q5792073|1=Machilus thunbergii}}, and {{ill|lt=Ilex latifolia|Q2706373|WD=Q2706373|1=Ilex latifolia}} grow densely within the grounds.<ref name="sk7"/>

=== Enshrined deity ===
The enshrined deity is {{ill|WD=Q595520|lt=Tsukuyomi|Q595520|WD=Q595520|WD=Q595520|lt=Tsukuyomi no Mikoto no Mitama|1=Tsukuyomi-no-Mikoto}}.<ref name="ic"/> This is the same {{ill|lt=deity (Shinto)|Q524158|WD=Q524158|lt=kami|1=Kami}} worshipped at the {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Tsukiyomi-no-miya|1=UNKNOWN}} and {{ill|lt=Tsukiyomi-no-miya (Yoru-no-miya)|Q11516023|WD=Q11516023|1=Tsukiyomi-no-Miya (Gekū)}}, among the 125 shrines of Ise Grand Shrine.<ref name="tm"/> While Tsukuyomi is generally regarded as a {{ill|lt=moon|Q405|WD=Q405|1=Moon}} deity, the moon and {{ill|lt=water|Q283|WD=Q283|1=Water}} are considered closely related, and in the context of Kawara Shrine, Tsukuyomi no Mikoto no Mitama is regarded as a river deity.<ref name="ic"/>

In addition to sharing the same enshrined deity, there is a theory that Sōchi-chō, where Kawara Shrine is located, and Nakamura-chō, where Tsukiyomi Shrine is located, were both deeply connected through the {{ill|lt=Arakida clan|Q11618716|WD=Q11618716|1=Arakida clan}}.<ref name="sk6"/>

=== History ===
According to tradition, {{ill|lt=Yamatohime-no-Mikoto|Q4388732|WD=Q4388732|1=Yamatohime-no-mikoto}} was welcomed here by Ōwakako-no-Mikoto and traveled down the Miyagawa River by boat.<ref name="ic"/> Regarding its establishment, based on the definition of auxiliary shrines of Ise Grand Shrine, it is believed to have been founded before the compilation of the *{{ill|lt=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|Q11064932|WD=Q11064932|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō}}*, that is, before the 5th year of {{ill|lt=Enchō (era)|Q1327274|WD=Q1327274|lt=Enchō|1=Enchō}} ({{ill|lt=927|Q30470|WD=Q30470|1=927}}).<ref name="ic2">Ise Bunka-sha, ed. (2008): p. 22</ref> According to the *Daijingū-shiki*, it was designated as one of the shrines involved in repairs by the {{ill|lt=chief priest|Q11453500|WD=Q11453500|1=Gūji}}, and it received offerings during the Kinensai and Kannamesai festivals.<ref name="sk5"/> However, during the {{ill|lt=Sengoku period (Japan)|Q204023|WD=Q204023|lt=Sengoku period|1=Sengoku period}}, {{ill|lt=rituals|Q11546824|WD=Q11546824|1=Saitan-sai}} were discontinued,<ref name="tm"/> and the shrine grounds fell into ruin.<ref name="sk5"/> The hereditary priests ({{ill|lt=hafuri|Q11453500|WD=Q11453500|lt=hafuri|1=Gūji}}) who had been appointed until then also died out.<ref name="sk6"/>

In the 3rd year of {{ill|lt=Kanbun|Q1146228|WD=Q1146228|1=Kanbun (era)}} ({{ill|lt=1663|Q7577|WD=Q7577|1=1663}}), {{ill|lt=Kawabe Kiyonaga|Q92924151|WD=Q92924151|lt=Kawabe Kiyonaga|1=Kiyonaga Kawabe}} restored the shrine at Sōchi.<ref name="sk5"/> On the 9th day of the 9th month (lunar calendar; {{ill|lt=Gregorian calendar|Q12138|WD=Q12138|1=Gregorian calendar}}: {{ill|lt=October 9|Q2923|WD=Q2923|1=October 9}}, 1663), the {{ill|lt=shrine buildings|Q11453500|WD=Q11453500|1=Gūji}} were constructed, and on the 12th day of the 12th month (lunar calendar; Gregorian: {{ill|lt=January 10|Q2243|WD=Q2243|1=January 10}}, {{ill|lt=1664|Q7581|WD=Q7581|1=1664}}), the deity was ceremonially transferred ({{ill|lt=sengu|Q24892914|WD=Q24892914|1=Sengyo}}).<ref name="sk5"/> At that time, families that had formerly served as hereditary priests were sought out and reinstated.<ref name="sk6"/> During the restoration, local traditions and place names were referenced, but there is also a theory that Takehana Shrine in present-day Takegahana-chō, Ise City, or {{ill|lt=Kawarabuchi Shrine|Q17209232|WD=Q17209232|1=Kawarabuchi Shrine}} in {{ill|lt=Fun'e (Ise City)|Q17213313|WD=Q17213313|1=Funae}} may have been the original site.<ref name="tm"/> Since a subsequent deity transfer on the 29th day of the 3rd month of the 7th year of {{ill|lt=Genroku|Q1325001|WD=Q1325001|1=Genroku}} (lunar calendar; Gregorian: {{ill|lt=April 23|Q2527|WD=Q2527|1=April 23}}, {{ill|lt=1694|Q7694|WD=Q7694|1=1694}}), the shrine site has not moved.<ref name="sk5"/>

The shrine buildings were rebuilt in November {{ill|lt=1908|Q2056|WD=Q2056|1=1908}} ({{ill|lt=Meiji|Q3125096|WD=Q3125096|1=Meiji era}} 41).<ref>Ujiyamada City Office, ed. (1929): p. 11</ref> They were subsequently rebuilt again in May {{ill|lt=1960|Q3754|WD=Q3754|1=1960}} ({{ill|lt=Shōwa|Q327567|WD=Q327567|1=Shōwa era}} 35), and repairs were carried out in September {{ill|lt=1983|Q2434|WD=Q2434|1=1983}} (Shōwa 58).<ref name="sk6"/>

=== Rituals ===
During the Kinensai, {{ill|lt=Tsukinamisai|Q11516161|WD=Q11516161|1=Tsukinami-no-Matsuri}} (June and December), {{ill|lt=Kannamesai|Q11255045|WD=Q11255045|1=Kannamesai Festival}}, {{ill|lt=Niinamesai|Q11501518|WD=Q11501518|1=Niiname-no-Matsuri}}, and extraordinary festivals, {{ill|lt=Shinto priests|Q60523|WD=Q60523|1=Kannushi}} present {{ill|lt=offerings|Q11591045|WD=Q11591045|lt=shinsen|1=Shinsen}} and {{ill|lt=heihaku|Q11481885|WD=Q11481885|1=Hanpei}} before the shrine. For rites such as the {{ill|lt=Saitan-sai|Q11546824|WD=Q11546824|1=Saitan-sai}}, remote worship is performed at the Inner Shrine’s Gojōden Hall.<ref name="sk6"/>

=== Access ===
The shrine is situated in a forest slightly off {{ill|lt=Mie Prefectural Route 22 (Ise–Minamishima Line)|Q11357908|WD=Q11357908|1=Mie Prefectural Road Route 22}}.<ref>Shikinaisha Research Association, ed. (1990): pp. 346–347</ref>

* About a 12-minute walk (700 {{ill|lt=meters|Q11573|WD=Q11573|lt=m|1=Metre}}) from the “Sōchi” {{ill|lt=bus stop|Q953806|WD=Q953806|1=Bus stop}} on a {{ill|lt=Mie Kōtsū|Q6843516|WD=Q6843516|1=Mie Kotsu}} {{ill|lt=route bus|Q1373492|WD=Q1373492|1=Transit bus}}.<ref name="ic"/> Enter the alley opposite {{ill|lt=Ise Municipal Sōchi Elementary School|Q11379036|WD=Q11379036|1=Ise Municipal Sōchi Elementary School}} near the bus stop and follow the rice paddies to reach the precincts.<ref name="ic"/>
* Approximately 5.5 {{ill|lt=kilometers|Q828224|WD=Q828224|lt=km|1=Kilometre}} from {{ill|lt=Ise-shi Station|Q6079748|WD=Q6079748|1=Iseshi Station}} on the {{ill|lt=JR Sangū Line|Q872023|WD=Q872023|1=Sangū Line}} of {{ill|lt=Central Japan Railway Company|Q513679|WD=Q513679|1=Central Japan Railway Company}} or the {{ill|lt=Kintetsu Yamada Line|Q900630|WD=Q900630|1=Yamada Line (Kintetsu)}} of {{ill|lt=Kintetsu|Q1531085|WD=Q1531085|1=Kintetsu Railway}}.<ref name="sk5"/>

